eplace_all": false, "file_path": "/home/axel/LeanAlgebraicGeometry-Horizon/MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge/AlgebraicJacobian/CohomControl_qcohRoute_oldRoute`; `g` being an\n1444:theorem leakWitness_qcohRoute_nonvacuous {R R' : CommRingCat.{u}} (\u03c6 : R \u27f6 R')\n1449:  leakProbe_qcohRoute_kernel (Spec.map \u03c6) (\ud835\udfd9 (tilde M)) inferInstance inferInstance\n1451:#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_kernel\n1452:#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_homologyIso\n1453:#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_coneCancel\n1454:#print axioms leakControl_qcohRoute_oldRoute\n1455:#print axioms leakWitness_qcohRoute_nonvacuous"}, "usage": null, "_cursor": 793329}, {"kind": "text", "at": "2026-07-28T12:41:14.647000+00:00", "text": "Notably absent: no probe measures `cech_flatBaseChange` itself. That's the declaration the task's headline is about. Let me add probes.", "tool": "", "data": {}, "usage": {"tokens_in": 84613, "tokens_out": 1, "cached_tokens_in": 0, "reasoning_tokens_out": 0, "cost_usd": null}, "_cursor": 795552}, {"kind": "tool_call", "at": "2026-07-28T12:41:15.525000+00:00", "text": "", "tool": "Read", "data": {"input": {"limit": 40, "offset": 1424, "file_path": "/home/axel/LeanAlgebraicGeometry-Horizon/MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge/scripts/axiom-frontier.lean"}}, "usage": {"tokens_in": 84613, "tokens_out": 1, "cached_tokens_in": 0, "reasoning_tokens_out": 0, "cost_usd": null}, "_cursor": 795898}, {"kind": "tool_result", "at": "2026-07-28T12:41:16.370000+00:00", "text": "", "tool": "", "data": {"content": "1424\tnoncomputable def leakControl_qcohRoute_oldRoute {S S' : Scheme.{u}} (g : S' \u27f6 S) [Flat g]\n1425\t    (K : CochainComplex S.Modules \u2115) (i : \u2115) :\n1426\t    (((Scheme.Modules.pullback g).mapHomologicalComplex (ComplexShape.up \u2115)).obj K).homology i\n1427\t      \u2245 (Scheme.Modules.pullback g).obj (K.homology i) :=\n1428\t  pullback_mapHC_homologyIso g K i\n1429\t\n1430\t/-- **NON-VACUITY WITNESS** for the two hypothesis-carrying probes above, per trap (c)/(d) of the\n1431\tworkspace catalogue: a clean axiom line proves nothing if the hypotheses cannot be satisfied.\n1432\t`leakProbe_qcohRoute_kernel` carries `A.IsQuasicoherent`, `B.IsQuasicoherent` as *named*\n1433\thypotheses and `[Flat g]`, `[IsAffine S]`, `[IsAffine S']` as instance binders \u2014 all four kinds\n1434\tthat the catalogue records as invisible to `#print axioms`.\n1435\t\n1436\tSo here the theorem is FIRED, at objects that exist: `A = B = M^~` over `S = S' = Spec R` (the\n1437\ttilde of any `R`-module is quasi-coherent, `AlgebraicGeometry.tilde` instance), with the map the\n1438\tidentity and `g` a `Spec` of a flat ring map.  If the hypotheses were unsatisfiable \u2014 or the\n1439\tstatement vacuous \u2014 this would not elaborate.\n1440\t\n1441\tNote what this does and does not do: it witnesses *satisfiability*, not strength.  The strength\n1442\tclaim rests on `leakProbe_qcohRoute_*` versus `leakControl_qcohRoute_oldRoute`; `g` being an\n1443\tidentity-like `Spec` map here would be the wrong argument for *that* comparison (\u00a76b). -/\n1444\ttheorem leakWitness_qcohRoute_nonvacuous {R R' : CommRingCat.{u}} (\u03c6 : R \u27f6 R')\n1445\t    (h\u03c6 : \u03c6.hom.Flat) (M : ModuleCat.{u} R) :\n1446\t    Limits.PreservesLimit (Limits.parallelPair (\ud835\udfd9 (tilde M)) 0)\n1447\t      (Scheme.Modules.pullback (Spec.map \u03c6)) :=\n1448\t  haveI : Flat (Spec.map \u03c6) := Flat.SpecMap_iff.mpr h\u03c6\n1449\t  leakProbe_qcohRoute_kernel (Spec.map \u03c6) (\ud835\udfd9 (tilde M)) inferInstance inferInstance\n1450\t\n1451\t#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_kernel\n1452\t#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_homologyIso\n1453\t#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_coneCancel\n1454\t#print axioms leakControl_qcohRoute_oldRoute\n1455\t#print axioms leakWitness_qcohRoute_nonvacuous\n1456\t\n1457\tend AlgebraicGeometry\n1458\t"}, "usage": null, "_cursor": 796283}, {"kind": "text", "at": "2026-07-28T12:41:19.577000+00:00", "text": "Now the measurement that answers the task's headline question. Let me add probes for the actual base-change theorems.", "tool": "", "data": {}, "usage": {"tokens_in": 84663, "tokens_out": 1, "cached_tokens_in": 0, "reasoning_tokens_out": 0, "cost_usd": null}, "_cursor": 798689}, {"kind": "tool_call", "at": "2026-07-28T12:41:43.497000+00:00", "text": "", "tool": "Edit", "data": {"input": {"replace_all": false, "file_path": "/home/axel/LeanAlgebraicGeometry-Horizon/MainProjects/Algebraic-Jacobian-Challenge/scripts/axiom-frontier.lean", "old_string": "#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_kernel\n#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_homologyIso\n#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_coneCancel\n#print axioms leakControl_qcohRoute_oldRoute\n#print axioms leakWitness_qcohRoute_nonvacuous\n\nend AlgebraicGeometry", "new_string": "#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_kernel\n#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_homologyIso\n#print axioms leakProbe_qcohRoute_coneCancel\n#print axioms leakControl_qcohRoute_oldRoute\n#print axioms leakWitness_qcohRoute_nonvacuous\n\n/-! ### \u00a76d. FLAT BASE CHANGE ITSELF \u2014 the declaration the task is about\n\nEverything in \u00a76c measures *ingredients*.  Until now **nothing in this file measured\n`cech_flatBaseChange`**, which is the theorem the whole `AJC.fbc` lane exists to prove \u2014 so the\nlane's axiom claims were being read off its inputs.  Fix that: the three declarations below are\nthe endpoint, its hypothesis-free form, and the two structural lemmas that removed the\ncosimplicial naturality obligation.\n\nRead the group as follows.\n\n* `leakEndpoint_cech_flatBaseChange` and `leakEndpoint_cech_flatBaseChange_qcoh` are both\n  expected to report `sorryAx`, and for a **single** reason: `cechComplex_baseChange_iso` carries\n  the two cosimplicial naturality `sorry`s of `cech_pushforward_baseChange_natIso` and\n  `twisted_cech_nerve_iso`.  That is now the *only* obstruction \u2014 the `_qcoh` form no longer\n  routes through flat exactness and no longer carries the `h\u2082`/`h\u2083` quasi-coherence hypotheses.\n* `leakProbe_cechTerm_isQuasicoherent` is the discharge of those hypotheses and must be **clean**.\n  If it ever reports `sorryAx`, `cech_flatBaseChange_qcoh` has silently regressed to depending on\n  something unproved *besides* naturality.\n* `leakProbe_whiskeredBC_natIso` is the structural half: the cosimplicial natural isomorphism\n  built by whiskering the outer mate.  It must be **clean**, and its cleanliness is the content\n  of the claim \"naturality is not an obligation\" \u2014 it constructs, from a degreewise `IsIso` alone,\n  the object that `NatIso.ofComponents` could only produce with a naturality proof.\n* `leakProbe_isIso_app_pi` is the reduction of that degreewise `IsIso` to one per index tuple.\n  Clean, and pure category theory.\n\nSo the honest summary of the lane is readable off four lines: the two endpoints dirty, the four\nsupporting reductions clean, and the delta between them is exactly the per-\u03c3 Beck\u2013Chevalley\ncomparison. -/\n\ntheorem leakProbe_cechTerm_isQuasicoherent {S X : Scheme.{u}} (f : X \u27f6 S) [IsSeparated f]\n    [X.IsSeparated] [IsAffine S] (\ud835\udcb0 : X.OpenCover) [Finite \ud835\udcb0.I\u2080]\n    (h\ud835\udcb0 : \u2200 i, IsAffine (\ud835\udcb0.X i)) (F : X.Modules) (hF : F.IsQuasicoherent) (p : \u2115) :\n    ((CechComplex f \ud835\udcb0 F).X p).IsQuasicoherent :=\n  isQuasicoherent_cechComplex_X f \ud835\udcb0 h\ud835\udcb0 F hF p\n\nnoncomputable def leakProbe_whiskeredBC_natIso {S S' X X' : Scheme.{u}}\n    (f : X \u27f6 S) (g : S' \u27f6 S) (f' : X' \u27f6 S') (g' : X' \u27f6 X)\n    (h : IsPullback g' f' f g) (\ud835\udcb0 : X.OpenCover) (F : X.Modules)\n    (hiso : \u2200 n : SimplexCategory, IsIso ((cechOuterBC f g f' g' h).app\n      ((CosimplicialObject.Augmented.drop.obj (CechNerve \ud835\udcb0 F)).obj n))) :\n    ((CosimplicialObject.whiskering S.Modules S'.Modules).obj\n     .\n11\t\n12\tThis skill is the first pass: four cheap reads, a guess at the situation, then\n13\tthe context that situation actually calls for. Budget a couple of minutes, not a\n14\ttenth of the session.\n15\t\n16\t## The four signals\n17\t\n18\t```bash\n19\tls \"$ARCHON_HORIZON_SESSION_DIR/..\"     # sibling sessions in THIS run\n20\t\"$HORIZON_BIN\" usage --json             # `paused` (why the last run stopped) + `recent_failure_reasons`\n21\t\"$HORIZON_BIN\" ps                       # is another run live on this workspace RIGHT NOW?\n22\t\"$HORIZON_GIT\" log --oneline -15        # what actually landed recently\n23\t```\n24\t\n25\tThe sibling listing is the one that decides most of it. A run's sessions look\n26\tlike:\n27\t\n28\t```\n29\t0001-system            \u2190 orchestrator bookkeeping (commits, integration), not an agent\n30\t0002-horizon-PET.5     \u2190 a horizon session; meta.json + transcript.jsonl + usage.json\n31\t0003-system\n32\t0004-horizon-PET.5     \u2190 ...you, probably\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\tFor any earlier `*-horizon-*` sibling, two files answer everything:\n36\t\n37\t- **`report.md` present** \u2192 that session finished on its own terms and wrote you\n38\t  a hand-off. **`report.md` absent** \u2192 it was killed mid-flight. This is the\n39\t  single most informative bit on disk.\n40\t- **`meta.json`** \u2192 `round`, `task_id`, `status`, `started_at`, `ended_at`,\n41\t  `model`. `ended_at` against the current time tells you whether \"recent\" means\n42\t  four minutes ago or last week.\n43\t\n44\t## Which situation am I in?\n45\t\n46\tWork down the list; the first match is your best guess.\n47\t\n48\t**An earlier horizon sibling has no `report.md`** \u2192 *resume after a crash or\n49\tkill.* Its work is half-done and possibly half-committed. Read its commits in\n50\tthe ledger first (they are the part that survived), then the tail of its\n51\ttranscript to see what it was in the middle of \u2014 `usage --json` `paused` gives\n52\tthe reason it died (usage limit, budget, auth), and `recent_failure_reasons`\n53\tshows rate-limit pressure that may still be there. Trust the ledger over the\n54\ttranscript: anything it did not commit is gone, no matter what it said it was\n55\tdoing. If the dead session shares your `ARCHON_HORIZON_TASK`, you are continuing\n56\tits exact work \u2014 pick up its front rather than opening a new one.\n57\t\n58\t**An earlier horizon sibling has a `report.md`** \u2192 *hand-off from the session\n59\tthat just finished.* The common case. Read that report first \u2014 its `## Next` and\n60\t`## Why I stopped` were written for exactly this moment \u2014 then its commits, then\n61\tany inbox items addressed to horizon since its `ended_at`. Do not re-derive what\n62\tit already established; do not redo a piece it says is done without checking the\n63\tledger.\n64\t\n65\t**`ARCHON_HORIZON_ROUND` is `0` and no horizon sibling exists** \u2192 *first agent\n66\tof the run.* Nobody handed you anything **in this run** \u2014 but the workspace is\n67\talmost never greenfield. Earlier runs live under `.archon-horizon/runs/`; the\n68\troadmap, the inbox and the ledger carry their conclusions. Skim the roadmap and\n69\trecent ledger history before deciding a strategy that a previous run already\n70\ttried and rejected.\n71\t\n72\t**`ARCHON_HORIZON_RUN` is unset** \u2192 *not launched by the orchestrator* (an\n73\tinteractive or hand-driven session). No run context exists to reconstruct;\n74\torient from workspace state \u2014 roadmap, inbox, ledger \u2014 and say where things\n75\tstand before proposing work.\n76\t\n77\tOne caveat about round numbers. `ARCHON_HORIZON_ROUND` is 0-based and reliable\n78\tfor \"am I first\" \u2014 but a resumed run can re-run a round whose session died, so a\n79\t`0` with a dead sibling present means *resume*, not *fresh*. The dead-sibling\n80\tcheck settles it either way. (`ROUND + 1 == ROUNDS` is a sound last-round test,\n81\tresumed runs included \u2014 `ROUNDS` bounds the rounds *this* invocation drives, not\n82\tthe run's original plan.)\n83\t\n84\t## Then, before you start\n85\t\n86\t- **`horizon ps` said another run is live?** You share one ledger branch with it.\n87\t  Commit in small coherent pieces and expect to see its commits interleaved.\n88\t- **Timestamps are context.** A predecessor that ended 90 seconds ago probably\n89\t  left a warm build; one that ended last week may have left a workspace that no\n90\t  longer compiles. Check rather than assume.\n91\t- **The inbox is the cross-session thread** \u2014 read what past sessions left for\n92\t  horizon, and the task's own comments if you share their `task_id`\n93\t  (`\"$HORIZON_BIN\" task show \"$ARCHON_HORIZON_TASK\" --json`). Skill:\n94\t  `horizon-inbox`.\n95\t\n96\tThen stop orienting and start working. The point of this pass is to begin from a\n97\tsmarter place than round zero \u2014 not to reconstruct everything that ever happened\n98\there.\n99\t"}, "usage": null, "_cursor": 26142}, {"kind": "tool_call", "at": "2026-07-25T08:19:50.617000+00:00", "text": "", "tool": "Bash", "data": {"input": {"command": "cd \"$ARCHON_HORIZON_ROOT\"; ls \"$ARCHON_HORIZON_SESSION_DIR/..\"; echo \"---PS---\"; \"$HORIZON_BIN\" ps 2>&1 | tail -20; echo \"---LOG---\"; \"$HORIZON_GIT\" log --oneline -20", "description": "Four orientation signals"}}, "usage": {"tokens_in": 34297, "tokens_out": 1, "cached_tokens_in": 0, "reasoning_tokens_out": 0, "cost_usd": null}, "_cursor": 31941}, {"kind": "tool_result", "at": "2026-07-25T08:19:51.826000+00:00", "text": "", "tool": "", "data": {"content": "0001-system\n0002-horizon-ajcr-w4-rep-free\n0003-system\n0004-horizon-ajcr-w4-rep-free\n0005-system\n0006-horizon-ajcr-w4-rep-free\n---PS---\n\n   ___           __               __ __         _             \n  / _ | ________/ /  ___  ___    / // /__  ____(_)__ ___  ___ \n / __ |/ __/ __/ _ \\/ _ \\/ _ \\  / _  / _ \\/ __/ /_ // _ \\/ _ \\\n/_/ |_/_/  \\__/_//_/\\___/_//_/ /_//_/\\___/_/ /_//__/\\___/_//_/\n\n v0.1.2    ht